152 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 266 0 R
/E (The algorithm divides the data into K distinct clusters, where K is a user-defined number. It works in the following steps:)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
153 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (      Randomly initialize K centroids \(one for each cluster\).       Assign each data point to the nearest centroid based on distance \(usually        Euclidean distance\).       Recalculate the centroids as the mean of all data points in a cluster.       Repeat steps 2 and 3 until the centroids no longer change significantly.)
/E (      Randomly initialize K centroids \(one for each cluster\).       Assign each data point to the nearest centroid based on distance \(usually        Euclidean distance\).       Recalculate the centroids as the mean of all data points in a cluster.       Repeat steps 2 and 3 until the centroids no longer change significantly.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 267 0 R
/E (The number of clusters, K, needs to be set manually. One commonly used method to find the optimal K is the Elbow Method, where we plot the Within-Cluster Sum of Squares \(WCSS\) against different values of K and choose the point where the rate of decrease sharply slows)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
